Based on your two project documents, I would build your website as a Chemistry Reaction Predictor.

The goal is to create a website where chemistry students can enter two reactants and their quantities, then receive a prediction of what will happen in the reaction.

I would keep the program at an introductory CISC 106 level while making sure it addresses the requirements in your design worksheet.

1. How your website will work
Your website will have six main pages and seven routes, following your design worksheet.

Page

Functionality

Home

Introduces the Chemistry Reaction Predictor.

Input

Allows users to enter two reactants and their amounts in moles.

Results

Displays the predicted reaction and calculations.

History

Displays previously calculated reactions.

Reaction Details

Displays the complete results of a previous reaction.

Learn

Explains the different types of chemical reactions.

The program will include:

Two dataclasses: State and ReactionResult.

Seven routes: Home, input, calculation, results, history, reaction details, and learning.

Five user input fields: Two reactants, two quantities, and reaction type.

At least four conditional statements for validating input and determining results.

A for loop that displays the reaction history.

Helper functions to make the chemistry calculations easier to understand.

One important design decision: rather than attempting to predict every possible chemical reaction, I recommend using a database of supported reactions. This makes your program much more manageable and reduces the likelihood of incorrect chemical predictions.

